.TH WARREN 1 2026-10-21 "warren" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
warren \- a colony of Claude Code and OMP agents from one terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B warren
.br
.B warren new
.I NAME
.RI [ DIR ]
.RI [ COLOR ]
.RB [ new | resume | continue ]
.RI [ SESSION ]
.RB [ \-\-kind=claude | omp ]
.RB [ \-\-sys=\c
.IR PROMPT ]
.RB [ \-\-extra=\c
.IR ARGS ]
.RB [ \-\-title=\c
.IR TITLE ]
.RB [ \-\-matrix | \-\-no\-matrix ]
.br
.B warren ls
.br
.B warren kill
.I NAME
.br
.B warren sleep
.I NAME
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.br
.B warren wake
.I NAME
.br
.B warren set
.I NAME
.RB [ \-\-dir=\c
.IR DIR ]
.RB [ \-\-extra=\c
.IR ARGS ]
.RB [ \-\-sys=\c
.IR PROMPT ]
.RB [ \-\-matrix=on | off ]
.br
.B warren join
.I NAME ROOM
.br
.B warren renew
.I NAME
.RB [ \-\-matrix=on | off ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.br
.B warren cycle
.RI [ NAME ...]
.RB [ \-\-renew ]
.br
.B warren attach
.I NAME
.br
.B warren restore
.RI [ NAME ]
.br
.B warren sessions
.RB [ claude | omp ]
.br
.B warren help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B warren
runs many coding agents at once, each in its own daemon that answers to no
terminal, and shows them in a dashboard: a sidebar of agents grouped by
directory, and the focused agent's own TUI beside it.
The dashboard holds no state; quitting it, or losing the SSH connection it
ran over, leaves every agent running, and
.B warren
again rebuilds the same view from the daemons.
.PP
An agent can be put to
.I sleep
(its harness process stopped, its tab kept) and woken into the same
conversation.
Agents on other machines join the same sidebar over ssh; see
.BR HOSTS .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B warren
Open the dashboard.
Agents this machine lost when it last stopped come back first, asleep.
.TP
.BI "warren new " NAME " " "\fR[\fPDIR\fR] [\fPCOLOR\fR] [\fPMODE\fR] [\fPSESSION\fR]"
Start an agent named
.I NAME
in
.I DIR
(default: the current directory; created if missing), with sidebar colour
.I COLOR
(0\(en255).
.I MODE
is
.B new
(the default),
.B resume
(with a
.I SESSION
id, or for OMP a session file), or
.BR continue .
.B \-\-kind
picks the harness,
.B \-\-sys
its system prompt,
.B \-\-extra
extra arguments for its command line, and
.B \-\-title
the name it starts under (for Claude Code, as if by
.BR /rename ).
.B \-\-matrix
and
.B \-\-no\-matrix
give it chat or not; without either, the
.B newAgents
setting decides (see
.BR CHAT ).
.TP
.B warren ls
List agents: slot, name, state and directory.
.TP
.BI "warren kill " NAME
End the agent.
It is not restored after a restart.
.TP
.BI "warren sleep " NAME
Stop the agent's harness process, and everything it started, to give its
memory back; keep the agent.
Refused while it is mid-turn, unless
.B \-\-force
is given, which loses that turn.
Refused before the agent has a session id, since there would be nothing to
resume.
.TP
.BI "warren wake " NAME
Start a sleeping agent again on the same conversation.
Refused if its directory no longer exists.
.TP
.BI "warren set " NAME
Change how the agent starts, from its next start on: the directory it runs
in
.RB ( \-\-dir ,
which must exist), its extra arguments
.RB ( \-\-extra ),
its system prompt
.RB ( \-\-sys ),
or whether it has chat
.RB ( \-\-matrix=on | off ).
An empty
.B \-\-extra=
or
.B \-\-sys=
clears it.
Nothing restarts; sleep and wake a running agent to apply it.
Agents whose daemon predates this command say so, and must be made again.
.TP
.BI "warren join " "NAME ROOM"
Invite the agent to a Matrix room
.RI ( #alias:server
or
.IR !room-id ),
from warren's own account, which must be in that room.
The agent must have claimed a chat name.
.TP
.BI "warren renew " NAME
Move the agent onto the warren this command runs from.
A daemon keeps the binary it was started from for as long as it lives, so
an upgrade reaches an agent only when its daemon is made again; this does
that without losing it.
The agent is put to sleep (refused mid-turn without
.BR \-\-force ),
its daemon is stopped as a shutdown would stop it, and it is restored from
its note by the new binary
\(em with chat on or off if
.B \-\-matrix
says so \(em then woken if it was awake.
Use it for agents whose daemon predates
.B warren set
or chat.
.TP
.B "warren cycle \fR[\fINAME\fR...] [\fB\-\-renew\fR]"
Restart agents (all of this machine's but the one running the command, if
none is named) so they pick up a change, each as soon as it is at rest: not
mid-turn, with no shell running under its harness (a background command or
monitor) and no Claude subagent that has written in the last 90 seconds, for
ten seconds straight.
It waits as long as that takes, saying who it is waiting on and why.
Each is slept and woken, or with
.B \-\-renew
renewed (asleep ones too), which an upgrade of warren itself needs.
Agents asleep are otherwise left alone: they load everything fresh on waking.
.TP
.BI "warren attach " NAME
View one agent raw, with no sidebar.
.B Ctrl\-\e
detaches.
.TP
.BR "warren restore " [\c
.IR NAME ]
Bring back agents this machine lost when it stopped, asleep.
The dashboard does this by itself on launch.
.TP
.BR "warren sessions " [ claude | omp ]
List resumable sessions, tab-separated: id, time, directory, title.
.SH THE DASHBOARD
The dashboard is modal.
.B CLAUDE
mode, the default, sends every key to the focused agent.
.B Ctrl\-Space
toggles
.BR NORMAL ,
where keys drive warren.
Editing a form is
.B EDIT
mode.
.SS Anywhere
.TP
.B Ctrl\-Space
Toggle NORMAL mode.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-\e
Detach (leave every agent running).
.TP
.B Ctrl\-Z
Sleep or wake the focused agent.
Never passed to the agent: a harness stopped by its terminal is one warren
cannot wake.
.SS NORMAL mode
.TP
.BR j " / " k ", arrows"
Focus the next or previous agent.
.TP
.IR "digit digit"
Jump to a folder, then the agent inside it.
.TP
.BR g " / " G
First or last agent.
.TP
.BR J " / " K
Move the focused agent down or up within its folder.
.TP
.B Shift+digit
Move the focused agent to that position in its folder.
.TP
.B n
New-agent form.
.TP
.B r
Rename the agent (pins the name against the harness's own title).
.TP
.BR e " / " c
Edit form: title and colour.
.TP
.B z
Sleep the agent, or wake it.
Refused mid-turn.
.TP
.B Z
Sleep it even mid-turn, losing the turn: for a stuck agent, a busy reading
that is wrong, or a harness that needs restarting.
.TP
.B x
Close the agent (asks first).
.TP
.BR i ", " a ", " l ", " Enter ", " Esc
Back to CLAUDE mode.
.TP
.B :
Command line; see below.
.SS Commands
.TP
.BR :q ", " :q!
Detach; or kill every agent and exit.
.TP
.BI :set " KEY VALUE"
For the focused agent, as
.B warren set
would, on whichever machine it lives:
.B dir
(relative to where the agent is now, or
.BR ~/ \&...),
.BR extra ,
or
.BR sys .
.B :set dir ~/Research/PLow
moves it there from its next wake;
.B :set matrix off
starts it without chat.
.B :set extra=
or
.B :set sys=
clears it.
.B :set
alone shows where the agent runs.
.TP
.BI :join " ROOM"
Invite the focused agent to a chat room, as
.B warren join
would.
.TP
.BI :color " INDEX\fR|\fP#RRGGBB"
Recolour the focused agent.
.TP
.B :restore
Bring back agents lost when the machine stopped.
.SS Mouse
Click a sidebar row to focus it, a folder heading to fold it, a machine
heading to reconnect.
The wheel over the sidebar cycles agents; clicks and the wheel over the pane
go to the agent, or scroll warren's own scrollback for a harness that does
not take the mouse.
Everything on a form is clickable.
.SH SIDEBAR
Agents are grouped into folders by the directory directly under home that
their working directory is in.
A bold row is ready for you, plain is working (and turning a spinner),
.B !
is blocked on a permission prompt,
.B *
finished while you were elsewhere, and
.B z
is asleep.
A slow dot means the turn is over but something the agent started is still
running: a background command or monitor, or a subagent.
.SH HOSTS
.I ~/.warren/hosts
lists other machines, one ssh destination per line, with an optional second
column giving the path to
.B warren
there (an ssh command does not get your login PATH):
.PP
.RS
.nf
servo   /Users/servo\-critter/.local/bin/warren
programchild@leilan  /etc/profiles/per\-user/programchild/bin/warren
.fi
.RE
.PP
Their agents appear under a heading per machine, over one shared ssh
connection each, and the new-agent form can start agents there.
Keys or an ssh agent are required: ssh runs in BatchMode.
The command-line commands act on this machine's agents only; the dashboard
is what spans machines.
.SH RESTARTS
Each agent keeps a note of itself in
.IR ~/.warren/notes/ ,
removed only when the agent is ended on purpose.
After the machine restarts, the notes left behind are the agents that were
lost, and the dashboard brings them back asleep, each waking into its
conversation.
An agent whose harness is stopped by a signal (as at shutdown) goes to sleep
and keeps its note; one that exits by itself, as with
.BR /exit ,
ends.
.PP
Claude Code deletes transcripts older than
.B cleanupPeriodDays
(30 days by default) at startup.
An agent left asleep longer than that has nothing to wake into; raise it in
.IR ~/.claude/settings.json .
.SH CHAT
With
.I ~/.warren/matrix.json
present, agents get Matrix chat: each runs its own copy of matrix-channel,
an MCP server that pushes messages into the session and offers
.BR reply ,
.BR react ,
.BR send_file ,
.BR fetch_messages ,
.BR download ,
.B set_avatar
and
.BR search .
Claude Code takes it as a channel
.RB ( \-\-dangerously\-load\-development\-channels ,
approved at each start); OMP takes it through an extension built into
warren, whose tools are named
.BR matrix_reply ,
and so on.
.PP
An agent has no account until it calls
.B claim_name
with a name it chooses, which makes
.IR @name.machine .
It joins no rooms by itself and accepts invites only from the
.B inviters
listed; it hears only
.B allowedUsers
and the members of the
.BR rosterRoom ,
which warren invites it to once it has a name.
Messages from roster members are marked untrusted, and more than
.B agentLoopCap
of them in a row in one room, with nothing from a listed person between,
are dropped.
A listed person can change that limit for one room from the chat:
.B !loopcap
.IR N ,
.B !loopcap off
(no limit) or
.B !loopcap default
(back to
.BR agentLoopCap ).
Every agent in the room that applied it reacts with a check mark, and the setting
outlasts restarts.
An agent that joins the room later starts at the default until the command is
sent again, unless the room carries an
.B institute.immanence.loop_cap
state event
.RB ( {"cap":\ N} )
set by a room admin.
In rooms a message must mention the agent unless
.B requireMention
is false; a room's
.B "\(dqroomMentions\(dq: false"
makes @room not count there.
An agent catches up on what it wasn't pushed with
.BR fetch_messages ,
whose
.B since
takes an event id, or
.B \(dqlast\(dq
for the room's read marker, which the read moves forward; its
.B mentions_me
and
.B starts_with
filter the messages read.
.PP
The keys:
.B channel
and
.B python
(what to run),
.B machine
(the last name),
.BR env ,
.BR homeserver ,
.BR allowedUsers ,
.BR inviters ,
.BR rosterRoom ,
.BR reserved ,
.BR agentLoopCap ,
.BR requireMention ,
.B newAgents
(do new agents get chat), and
.B warrenAccount
(a file with warren's own
.B access_token
and
.BR homeserver ,
used for invites).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B WARREN_HOME
warren's directory (default
.IR ~/.warren ).
.TP
.B WARREN_SCROLLBACK
Lines of scrollback kept per agent for harnesses that do not keep their own
(default 2000).
.TP
.B WARREN_SSH
A command to use in place of ssh for other machines.
.TP
.B WARREN_LOG
A file each daemon appends a debug log to.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.warren/run/NAME.sock
One unix socket per live agent.
.TP
.I ~/.warren/run/NAME.err
Why an agent's daemon died before it was listening; read and removed by
whoever was waiting for it.
.TP
.I ~/.warren/notes/NAME.json
What an agent was, for restoring it.
.TP
.I ~/.warren/hooks.json
Claude Code hook settings, regenerated on spawn.
.TP
.I ~/.warren/hosts
Other machines.
.TP
.I ~/.warren/matrix.json
Chat setup; see
.BR CHAT .
.TP
.I ~/.warren/matrix/NAME/
One agent's chat channel: config, sync state, identity, and
.IR channel.log .
Archived under
.I .ended/
when the agent ends.
.TP
.I ~/.warren/ssh/
Shared ssh connection sockets.
.PP
warren only reads
.I ~/.claude
and
.IR ~/.omp .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR claude (1),
.BR ssh (1)
.PP
https://github.com/samuelmarquis/warren
